WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.180 --> 00:00:05.960
He started out as Robin, but over the past
80 years, Dick Grayson has evolved in a way

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.920
that very few other comic book characters
have.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:12.500
From the way that he redefined comics in the
Golden Age to the heroic haircut that he finally

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:16.180
ditched in the '90s, here's the truth behind
the evolution of Nightwing.

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.480
Nightwing's origins start all the way back
at the dawn of the Golden Age of Comics and

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:26.020
the explosion of new superheroes had hit the
page after Superman debuted in 1938 and became

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:28.160
a ridiculously profitable hit.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:33.460
Rather than trying to shoulder into the already
crowded field of costumed superhumans (look

00:00:33.460 --> 00:00:36.780
up Stardust the Super Wizard sometime if you
want to see how weird that side of things

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.219
was getting) Bill Finger brought artist Bob
Kane the idea of a costumed hero without superpowers

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.899
who was a little bit darker than the others.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:45.880
The character was called Batman.

00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:47.320
You've probably heard of him.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:52.080
The thing is, the Batman who debuted in 1940
wasn't quite the pop culture icon that he'd

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:52.580
become.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:56.680
Instead, the first few Batman stories are
really just ripping off the Shadow, a popular

00:00:56.780 --> 00:01:02.020
pulp novel and radio character, but with pointy
ears and a bat-winged cape instead of a slouch

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:03.460
hat and a trenchcoat.

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:08.520
Over the next few months, however, Finger,
Kane, and artist Jerry Robinson would refine

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:09.280
their character.

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.540
If you need proof that they were just figuring
things out as they went, consider that Batman

00:01:13.549 --> 00:01:19.749
didn't even have an origin story until Detective
Comics #33, a full six months after he made

00:01:19.749 --> 00:01:20.800
his first appearance.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.920
It was in Detective Comics #38, however, that
those creators added the final ingredient

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.380
for making Batman the character we can still
recognize today.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.490
On a cover that announced him as "The Sensational
Character Find of 1940", which, in retrospect,

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.900
is maybe the most accurate a comic book blurb
has ever been, Robin was introduced to the

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:39.400
world.

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:45.560
Sidekicks had existed before Robin, but Dick
Grayson the circus acrobat turned boy vigilante,

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:46.380
redefined the role.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:51.020
He was wildly popular with kids in the audience
who already loved Batman and were excited

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.020
about having a character their age who got
to hang out with their favorite character

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.100
and be his best friend.

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.979
All things considered, it was a much more
pleasant power fantasy than Batman himself,

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.000
who watched his parents die in an alley.

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:16.340
Sure, Robin's parents died too, but not only
did he get a new dad, his new dad was Batman.

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:20.340
No offense to the Flying Graysons, but that's
almost always going to be an upgrade.

00:02:20.349 --> 00:02:24.930
Before long, the sidekick population exploded
in the same way that superheroes had boomed

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:25.620
after Superman.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:30.940
Marvel's original Human Torch teamed up with
Toro a few months after Robin, giving readers

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:36.280
the dubious image of a man on fire being accompanied
into battle by a child who was also on fire.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.540
By the time Captain America debuted alongside
Bucky Barnes in 1941, a kid sidekick was virtually

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:46.880
mandatory for the genre, but none of them
could hold a candle to Robin's popularity.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:53.780
While he'd always be best known as the back
half of "Batman And," it was clear from the

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:56.829
beginning that Robin was every bit as popular
as his mentor.

00:02:56.829 --> 00:03:01.680
For proof, just look at the comics: it's nearly
impossible to find a Batman story in the '40s

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.910
or '50s where he wasn't hanging out with the
Boy Wonder, but Robin had plenty of solo stories,

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:09.660
even headlining Star-Spangled Comics for five
years.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.760
That means that if you do the math, Robin
had more appearances in the Golden Age than

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.460
Batman did.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:19.750
By the early '80s, he was even the star of
DC's hottest book, The New Teen Titans, a

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:24.209
revival of a series where a bunch of kid sidekicks
were thrown together for wacky adventures

00:03:24.209 --> 00:03:27.560
that had been reimagined as an X-Men-esque
superhero soap opera.

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:31.280
Finally, someone thought to ask the question
that had been hanging over Grayson's head

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:33.520
for at least a couple of decades by that point.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:37.840
If Dick Grayson can work just as well on his
own, then does he need to be Robin?

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.150
The answer, it turned out, was no, but that
also led to another question: what's he going

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:42.800
to be now?

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:47.360
One of the most interesting things about Nightwing
as a legacy is that it doesn't actually start

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:50.640
with Batman, at least, not in the way that
you might think.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:55.740
Instead, Dick Grayson inherited that heroic
identity from a somewhat unexpected place:

00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:00.080
Kal-El of Krypton, or as he's known to his
coworkers over at the Daily Planet, Clark

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:00.580
Kent.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:04.480
"Who are you?"

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:05.720
"A friend."

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:09.260
Oh, he's also Superman, but you probably knew
that.

00:04:09.269 --> 00:04:12.639
What you might not know is that he has another
heroic identity, too.

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:18.360
Back in 1963, Superman and Jimmy Olsen took
a vacation to the shrunken Kryptonian city.

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:23.199
Since Kandor replicated the conditions of
Krypton, Superman didn't have any of his powers

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:26.639
while he was there, but when he discovered
that he and Jimmy still needed to fight some

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.840
bottle crime, they created a pair of new identities
inspired by their friends in Gotham City:

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.720
Nightwing and Flamebird.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:39.180
In later versions of the story, Nightwing
was a Kryptonian folk hero who was essentially

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:43.200
that world's equivalent of Batman, and Dick
learned about him when Superman told him the

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:43.700
story.

00:04:44.300 --> 00:04:48.260
In both versions, the outcome was the same:
after leaving behind the identity of Robin,

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:52.420
and paving the way for that role to be filled
by other characters, establishing the idea

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:57.220
that Robin was a legacy to be passed down
rather than a static, permanent identity,

00:04:57.220 --> 00:05:02.420
Dick Grayson adopted the identity of Nightwing
as his own, complete with a wild new costume.

00:05:04.190 --> 00:05:08.289
After he briefly took over for Batman when
Bruce Wayne was recovering from the back injury

00:05:08.289 --> 00:05:12.800
he'd suffered at the hands of Bane, Dick Grayson
finally took the step that, going forward,

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.020
would define him as a character: he cut the
mullet he'd been rocking since the late '80s.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.431
Seriously, look at this hair.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:19.431
It had to go.

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:24.280
Oh, and he also got his own series, which
would run for over 150 issues.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:27.520
In a lot of ways, the launch of Nightwing
was when Dick Grayson became the standalone

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:30.780
character he'd been on the verge of becoming
since 1947.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.040
He hadn't been Robin for well over a decade
at that point, and there had, in fact, been

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:39.260
two other Robins in the intervening years,
but he'd always been seen in the context of

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:41.760
other characters, mainly as a member of the
Titans.

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.860
Now, though, things were a little different.

00:05:43.930 --> 00:05:48.200
Dick was still a member of both the Batman
family and the Titans, but it was at a remove

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:49.410
that allowed him to flourish.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:53.770
He got his own city to protect in the form
of Blüdhaven, a hilariously named exurb of

00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:57.880
Gotham that had all the crime but none of
the vigilantes, and developed his own roster

00:05:57.880 --> 00:05:58.740
of enemies.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.640
He also got a streamlined, minimalist costume
that stands as one of the greatest designs

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.880
of the '90s, and still exists, with a few
minor alterations, today.

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:11.440
Nightwing isn't the only other identity that
Dick Grayson adopted after leaving behind

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:13.420
his 40-year career as Robin.

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:17.260
In fact, the most surprising thing might be
that he never went back to being Robin at

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:17.760
all.

00:06:18.120 --> 00:06:21.720
Instead, Dick stepped up after Bruce Wayne
was apparently killed in an event called Final

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:22.420
Crisis.

00:06:23.020 --> 00:06:27.160
Needless to say, Bruce wasn't actually killed,
and was instead sent back in time to caveman

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:32.430
days before he clawed his way back through
the time-stream while being pursued by a techno-demonic

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:36.860
"Hyper-Adapter" that was once summoned through
a Satanic ritual by Thomas Jefferson.

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:38.480
You know, comic book stuff.

00:06:38.800 --> 00:06:43.200
Anyway, while all that was going on, Dick
took on the identity of Batman, while Bruce's

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.200
son Damian, who had been raised by the League
of Assassins, became the new Robin.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.060
Dick and Damian's tenure inverted the classic
dynamic of the duo, with Robin being the violent

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:55.580
grump and Batman being more friendly and approachable.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.180
Once Bruce returned, Dick went through another
career change.

00:06:59.540 --> 00:07:03.760
After his identity was revealed to the world,
Dick signed up with a multinational secret

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:08.060
intelligence agency called Spyral, where he
went on sexy adventures that answered the

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:11.940
question of what James Bond would be like
if he lived in the same universe as, say,

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:12.440
the Riddler.

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:16.760
Eventually, however, he found his way back
to Blüdhaven and the role of Nightwing...just

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:21.860
in time to be shot in the head, get amnesia,
and emerge as "Ric Grayson," presumably because

00:07:21.860 --> 00:07:26.700
DC Comics had finally hit their breaking point
after 80 years of jokes about his name.

00:07:28.479 --> 00:07:33.970
While Dick Grayson has been seen on the screen
since the first Batman film serial in 1943,

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.949
DC Universe's Titans marks the first time
that his identity as Nightwing has ever been

00:07:38.949 --> 00:07:40.720
seen in a live-action project.

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.580
That almost wasn't the case, though.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:47.160
You might not remember it because it was overshadowed
by all of Arnold's extremely cool ice puns…

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.940
"Alright everyone, chill."

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:56.819
...but part of the plot of 1997's Batman and
Robin is built around Dick Grayson's frustration

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.720
with his role as a sidekick and his desire
to strike out on his own.

00:08:00.340 --> 00:08:04.430
That makes sense, considering that this particular
version of Dick Grayson appears to have been

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:09.069
adopted by Bruce Wayne in his mid-20s, but
mostly, it's an excuse for him to be tempted

00:08:09.069 --> 00:08:10.180
away by Poison Ivy.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:14.960
In the novelization, however, it's mentioned
that Dick is constantly switching up his identity

00:08:14.979 --> 00:08:19.710
and costumes in an effort to find something
new, and at one point, he suggests "Nightwing"

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:22.640
as a potential codename before being shot
down by Bruce.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:27.500
That didn't make it into the film, but Robin's
infamously nippled costume there definitely

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:31.000
borrows most of its elements from the Nightwing
suit, replacing the previous movie's take

00:08:31.000 --> 00:08:33.500
on the contemporary '90s Robin costume.

00:08:33.910 --> 00:08:37.840
Also, while it wasn't live-action, it's worth
noting that Nightwing had been featured on

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:39.220
TV shows before as well.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.020
When Batman: The Animated Series was redesigned
as The New Batman Adventures, Dick Grayson

00:08:44.020 --> 00:08:48.290
became Nightwing, complete with mullet, and
the transition from Robin to Nightwing was

00:08:48.290 --> 00:08:50.920
even part of the Young Justice animated series
as well.

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:55.820
Nightwing has also been seen occasionally
on Teen Titans Go, also with the mullet.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.500
We just can't get rid of that thing, can we?

00:08:58.660 --> 00:09:02.620
On DC Universe's Titans, Dick Grayson's arc
as a character began with an attempt to move

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:07.800
out of Batman's considerable shadow and past
his role as Robin, and in the second season

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:12.240
finale, he finally makes the move that he's
been building to the entire series.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:15.280
This time, however, he doesn't take his inspiration
from Superman.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:19.900
Instead, the shift to Nightwing truly begins
when Dick allows himself to be arrested, isolating

00:09:19.900 --> 00:09:23.980
himself from the rest of the Titans in an
effort to keep the team away from the danger

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.240
posed by their longtime arch-nemesis, Deathstroke
the Terminator.

00:09:27.700 --> 00:09:32.070
While he's locked up and mired in a deep depression,
he finds a purpose in helping out a couple

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:36.900
of his cellmates, a pair of undocumented immigrants
in danger of being deported and killed once

00:09:36.900 --> 00:09:37.520
they get home.

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:41.640
From them, Dick hears about Alazul, a legendary
figure from their village.

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:42.920
"You know Alazul?

00:09:44.580 --> 00:09:46.000
In our village, Alazul's our hero.

00:09:47.000 --> 00:09:50.500
He lives between the moon and the stars, and
in the time of greatest need, he flies down

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:51.500
and saves you."

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:56.010
Realizing that they're talking about something
that sounds awfully familiar to a nocturnal

00:09:56.010 --> 00:10:00.270
bird-themed vigilante, Dick decides that this
is the new inspiration he needs to get back

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:04.970
in the hero game, escaping from prison and
getting a brand new cape-free costume.

00:10:04.970 --> 00:10:09.220
With that, his evolution into Nightwing is
complete, but as longtime fans are no doubt

00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:13.380
wondering, only time will tell if this Dick
Grayson will ever get the one true element

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:18.680
that separates Robin from Nightwing, the sidekick
from the hero: that sweet, sweet power mullet.

